{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user is running a cobbler-scaffold stitch task (#5421) to implement `cmd/timeout` for the go-unix-utils project. The specific scope is SRD063 requirements R1.1 through R1.4 (core timeout behavior only). Two files must be created: `cmd/timeout/main.go` (implementation) and `cmd/timeout/timeout_test.go` (differential tests against `gtimeout`). The acceptance criteria require: successful compilation, go vet passing, tests passing (or gracefully skipping if gtimeout isn't available), and all four R1.1-R1.4 requirements implemented with differential tests.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - GNU timeout reimplementation in Go (run a command with a time limit, kill with SIGTERM if it exceeds duration)\n   - Duration parsing: fractional values (0.5), suffix multipliers (s=seconds default, m=minutes, h=hours, d=days)\n   - Duration 0 means no time limit (command runs indefinitely)\n   - Differential testing via `pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests` comparing against `gtimeout` (Homebrew GNU coreutils)\n   - SIGPIPE handler via `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()`\n   - Exit code 124 for timeout, pass-through of command's exit code otherwise\n   - Exit codes 125 (internal error), 126/127 (command execution failure)\n   - Process group management via `os/exec` and `SysProcAttr.Setpgid`\n   - Task is R1.1-R1.4 ONLY — R2.x (signal selection, kill-after, foreground, preserve-status) are NOT in scope\n   - Non-goals: no --verbose, no platform-specific process group management beyond Go's SysProcAttr.Setpgid\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd063-timeout.yaml` (READ)\n     - This is the SRD defining all requirements for cmd/timeout\n     - R1.1: Run COMMAND with ARGS, kill with SIGTERM if exceeds DURATION\n     - R1.2: Support fractional durations (e.g., 0.5)\n     - R1.3: Support suffix multipliers: s (seconds, default), m (minutes), h (hours), d (days)\n     - R1.4: DURATION 0 means no time limit\n     - R2.1-R2.4: Signal selection, kill-after, foreground, preserve-status (NOT in scope)\n     - R3.1-R3.4: Exit codes (inherently needed but not explicitly in task scope)\n     - R4.1-R4.4: Differential testing requirements\n     - Non-goals: no --verbose, no platform-specific process group management beyond SysProcAttr.Setpgid\n   \n   - `cmd/timeout/main.go` (TO CREATE)\n     - timeout implementation with duration parsing, command execution, SIGTERM on timeout\n   \n   - `cmd/timeout/timeout_test.go` (TO CREATE)\n     - Differential tests against gtimeout reference binary\n\n   - Key existing packages referenced (all provided inline in prompt):\n     - `pkg/sys/sigpipe.go`: `InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` — must call at start of main()\n     - `pkg/testutils/build.go`: `BuildBinary(t, \".\")` — compiles cmd package for testing\n     - `pkg/testutils/difftest.go`: `RunDiffTests()`, `DiffTest` struct — differential test harness\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - No errors encountered yet — only the SRD was read before the summary was requested.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Read the SRD to understand the full specification for timeout\n   - Identified that while the task scopes to R1.1-R1.4, proper exit code handling (R3.x) is inherently needed for a functional implementation and testable differential tests\n   - R2.x features (--signal, --kill-after, --foreground, --preserve-status) are explicitly NOT in scope for this task\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - The initial message: A massive stitch task prompt containing the full project context, SRDs, source code, execution constitution, go style constitution, task description for implementing cmd/timeout R1.1-R1.4, constraints, design decisions, and acceptance criteria.\n   - \"CRITICAL: Respond with TEXT ONLY. Do NOT call any tools.\" — requesting this summary.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Create `cmd/timeout/main.go` implementing R1.1-R1.4:\n     - Duration parsing with fractional values and suffix multipliers (s, m, h, d)\n     - Command execution with time limit\n     - SIGTERM on timeout\n     - Duration 0 = no limit\n     - sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at start of main()\n     - Proper exit codes (124 on timeout, pass-through otherwise, 125/126/127 for errors)\n   - Create `cmd/timeout/timeout_test.go` with differential tests against gtimeout\n   - Run `go build -o bin/timeout ./cmd/timeout/` to verify compilation\n   - Run `go vet ./cmd/timeout/...` to verify no warnings\n   - Run `go test ./cmd/timeout/... -count=1` to verify tests pass\n\n8. Current Work:\n   I had just finished reading the SRD file (`docs/specs/software-requirements/srd063-timeout.yaml`) and was about to plan and implement the two files. No code has been written yet. The implementation plan based on the SRD and task requirements:\n   \n   For `cmd/timeout/main.go`:\n   - Parse arguments: DURATION COMMAND [ARG...]\n   - Parse duration string: support fractional values, suffix multipliers (s/m/h/d), duration 0 = no limit\n   - Execute command via os/exec with process group (Setpgid=true for non-foreground mode)\n   - Use context.WithTimeout or time.AfterFunc to enforce the time limit\n   - Send SIGTERM to process group on timeout\n   - Exit 124 on timeout, pass through command's exit code otherwise\n   - Exit 125 for internal errors, 126 for permission denied, 127 for command not found\n   \n   For `cmd/timeout/timeout_test.go`:\n   - Use testutils.BuildBinary(t, \".\") and exec.LookPath(\"gtimeout\")\n   - t.Skip if gtimeout not found\n   - Test cases: command completes before timeout, fractional duration timeout, suffix durations, duration 0 no-limit, error cases\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Write both `cmd/timeout/main.go` and `cmd/timeout/timeout_test.go` in full, then build and test. Per the go_style_constitution's implementation_strategy: \"Write all source files before running any build or test commands.\" I should write main.go first (with duration parsing, command execution, timeout logic, exit code handling, SIGPIPE handler), then the test file, then run go build, go vet, and go test once each.\n   \n   The task description states: \"Follow these steps in order. Complete each step before moving to the next. 1. Review provided context... 2. Plan approach... 3. Implement... 4. Verify...\"\n   \n   I completed step 1 (reviewed context including reading the SRD) and was beginning step 2 (planning approach) when the summary was requested.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/pdiddy/.claude/projects/-private-var-folders-dl-cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn-T-go-unix-utils-worktrees-5421/90c15c96-339f-482a-9f6f-05c431a9e14f.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"90c15c96-339f-482a-9f6f-05c431a9e14f","uuid":"3c3f0c1e-5beb-46c8-99e7-98927de13411","timestamp":"2026-05-21T14:29:41.244Z","isSynthetic":true}
